{"content_hash": "1271ea7c75c47f35", "version_id": 574, "article_id": 58, "level_code": "en_b2", "lang": "en", "level": 4, "level_label": "B2 中高级", "title": "Nikon strips prize from AI-made microscope video", "title_zh": "尼康取消AI生成显微视频的获奖资格", "has_translation": true, "topic": "tech", "source": "bbc_tech", "source_url": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cr86z33pdy9vo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss", "image": {"type": "photo", "url": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1614036417651-efe5912149d8?ixid=M3wxMDk3Mzk1fDB8MXxzZWFyY2h8MXx8cHJpemUlMjB3aW5uaW5nJTIwaW1hZ2V8ZW58MHwwfHx8MTc5MTc2NTYxNXww&ixlib=rb-4.1.0&w=1600&q=75&fm=jpg&fit=max", "provider": "unsplash", "credit": "Photo by Brands&People on Unsplash", "author": "Brands&People", "credit_url": "https://unsplash.com/@brandsandpeople?utm_source=daily-english-news&utm_medium=referral", "source": "Unsplash", "source_url": "https://unsplash.com/photos/yellow-paper-award-ribbon-ZdqSuxl3Lak?utm_source=daily-english-news&utm_medium=referral", "fallback": {"type": "gradient", "from": "hsl(16, 42%, 62%)", "to": "hsl(54, 46%, 48%)", "label": "TECH", "seed": "cec84960fed8"}}, "published_date": "2026-10-12", "word_count": 239, "reading_minutes": 1.2, "lead": "A scientist has lost a top photography prize after Nikon decided his winning video was made with generative AI. The company said the entry broke competition rules, while experts who raised the alarm say they knew it was fake.", "lead_zh": "一位科学家失去了重要的摄影奖项，因为尼康认定他的获奖视频是用生成式人工智能制作的。尼康表示该作品违反了比赛规则，而最先提出质疑的专家称他们一眼就看出这是假的。", "preview": "Nikon has disqualified the winner of its Small World In Motion contest after other scientists said the video was fake. The company said usin", "preview_zh": "尼康取消了其“Small World In Motion”摄影比赛的冠军资格，此前有其他科学家指出该视频是假的。尼康表示，在作品构成中使用生成式人工智能违反了比赛规则，该比赛只接受用高倍显微镜拍摄的照片和视频。尼康补充说，这一决定并非对参赛者职业声誉或科学工作的评判。", "has_audio": true, "audio": {"id": 559, "url": "https://pub-aba43a6fb1db4dc08fede1dbc81f3241.r2.dev/audio/58/en_b2.mp3", "duration": 95.475, "size_bytes": 576720, "engine": "edge-tts", "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural", "word_count": 266, "has_timeline": true}, "detail_url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@f76eeb6/content/data/versions/574.json", "paragraphs": ["Nikon has disqualified the winner of its Small World In Motion contest after other scientists said the video was fake. The company said using generative AI in the composition broke the rules of the competition, which accepts photos and videos taken with powerful microscopes. Nikon added that the decision was not a judgement of the entrant's professional reputation or scientific work.", "The winning entry came from Dr Ning Xu of Tsinghua University in China. He submitted a short video showing tiny, hair-like structures called cilia moving in the airway of a child with the respiratory condition PCD. Xu acknowledged using AI but said he believed he had followed the rules, and he cooperated with Nikon's investigation. The BBC has approached him for comment.", "Several scientists, including former competition judges, complained to Nikon on LinkedIn that the video did not look real. Dr Robert Hirst, a scientist at the University of Leicester, said he was not surprised and knew immediately that the video was fabricated. He has spent 20 years diagnosing PCD by studying cilia waveform, length, cell size and shape.", "Hirst, who leads the NHS centre for PCD diagnosis at the university, said the cells and cilia looked nothing like those from real patients. He added that the controversy had affected many patients, scientists, doctors and support networks worldwide. Nikon said it would now review the rules and procedures of the annual contest, which has run for 15 years."], "paragraphs_zh": ["尼康取消了其“Small World In Motion”摄影比赛的冠军资格，此前有其他科学家指出该视频是假的。尼康表示，在作品构成中使用生成式人工智能违反了比赛规则，该比赛只接受用高倍显微镜拍摄的照片和视频。尼康补充说，这一决定并非对参赛者职业声誉或科学工作的评判。", "获奖作品来自中国清华大学的徐宁博士。他提交了一段短视频，显示一名患有呼吸道疾病PCD的儿童气道中细小的毛发状结构——纤毛——在运动。徐宁承认使用了人工智能，但表示他认为自己遵守了规则，并配合了尼康的调查。BBC已联系他置评。", "包括前比赛评委在内的多位科学家在领英上向尼康投诉，称该视频看起来不真实。莱斯特大学的科学家罗伯特·赫斯特博士表示他并不惊讶，并立即知道视频是伪造的。他花了20年时间通过研究纤毛波形、长度、细胞大小和形状来诊断PCD。", "赫斯特是该大学NHS PCD诊断中心的负责人，他说这些细胞和纤毛与真实患者的完全不像。他补充说，这一争议影响了全球许多患者、科学家、医生和支持网络。尼康表示，现在将审查这项已举办15年的年度比赛的规则和程序。"], "body": "Nikon has disqualified the winner of its Small World In Motion contest after other scientists said the video was fake. The company said using generative AI in the composition broke the rules of the competition, which accepts photos and videos taken with powerful microscopes. Nikon added that the decision was not a judgement of the entrant's professional reputation or scientific work.\n\nThe winning entry came from Dr Ning Xu of Tsinghua University in China. He submitted a short video showing tiny, hair-like structures called cilia moving in the airway of a child with the respiratory condition PCD. Xu acknowledged using AI but said he believed he had followed the rules, and he cooperated with Nikon's investigation. The BBC has approached him for comment.\n\nSeveral scientists, including former competition judges, complained to Nikon on LinkedIn that the video did not look real. Dr Robert Hirst, a scientist at the University of Leicester, said he was not surprised and knew immediately that the video was fabricated. He has spent 20 years diagnosing PCD by studying cilia waveform, length, cell size and shape.\n\nHirst, who leads the NHS centre for PCD diagnosis at the university, said the cells and cilia looked nothing like those from real patients. He added that the controversy had affected many patients, scientists, doctors and support networks worldwide. Nikon said it would now review the rules and procedures of the annual contest, which has run for 15 years.", "body_zh": "尼康取消了其“Small World In Motion”摄影比赛的冠军资格，此前有其他科学家指出该视频是假的。尼康表示，在作品构成中使用生成式人工智能违反了比赛规则，该比赛只接受用高倍显微镜拍摄的照片和视频。尼康补充说，这一决定并非对参赛者职业声誉或科学工作的评判。\n\n获奖作品来自中国清华大学的徐宁博士。他提交了一段短视频，显示一名患有呼吸道疾病PCD的儿童气道中细小的毛发状结构——纤毛——在运动。徐宁承认使用了人工智能，但表示他认为自己遵守了规则，并配合了尼康的调查。BBC已联系他置评。\n\n包括前比赛评委在内的多位科学家在领英上向尼康投诉，称该视频看起来不真实。莱斯特大学的科学家罗伯特·赫斯特博士表示他并不惊讶，并立即知道视频是伪造的。他花了20年时间通过研究纤毛波形、长度、细胞大小和形状来诊断PCD。\n\n赫斯特是该大学NHS PCD诊断中心的负责人，他说这些细胞和纤毛与真实患者的完全不像。他补充说，这一争议影响了全球许多患者、科学家、医生和支持网络。尼康表示，现在将审查这项已举办15年的年度比赛的规则和程序。", "text": "Nikon has disqualified the winner of its Small World In Motion contest after other scientists said the video was fake. The company said using generative AI in the composition broke the rules of the competition, which accepts photos and videos taken with powerful microscopes. Nikon added that the decision was not a judgement of the entrant's professional reputation or scientific work. The winning entry came from Dr Ning Xu of Tsinghua University in China. He submitted a short video showing tiny, hair-like structures called cilia moving in the airway of a child with the respiratory condition PCD. Xu acknowledged using AI but said he believed he had followed the rules, and he cooperated with Nikon's investigation. The BBC has approached him for comment. Several scientists, including former competition judges, complained to Nikon on LinkedIn that the video did not look real. Dr Robert Hirst, a scientist at the University of Leicester, said he was not surprised and knew immediately that the video was fabricated. He has spent 20 years diagnosing PCD by studying cilia waveform, length, cell size and shape. Hirst, who leads the NHS centre for PCD diagnosis at the university, said the cells and cilia looked nothing like those from real patients. He added that the controversy had affected many patients, scientists, doctors and support networks worldwide. Nikon said it would now review the rules and procedures of the annual contest, which has run for 15 years.", "vocab": [{"word": "disqualify", "pos": "verb", "zh": "取消资格", "note": "常用被动语态，如 be disqualified from", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "generative AI", "pos": "noun", "zh": "生成式人工智能", "note": "指能生成文本、图像、视频的AI", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "backlash", "pos": "noun", "zh": "强烈反对", "note": "常与 spark 连用：spark a backlash", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "entrant", "pos": "noun", "zh": "参赛者", "note": "来自动词 enter", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "reputation", "pos": "noun", "zh": "声誉", "note": "常用搭配：professional reputation", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "submit", "pos": "verb", "zh": "提交", "note": "submit an entry / a video", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "respiratory", "pos": "adj", "zh": "呼吸的", "note": "respiratory condition 呼吸道疾病", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "acknowledge", "pos": "verb", "zh": "承认", "note": "比 admit 更正式", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "fabricate", "pos": "verb", "zh": "伪造", "note": "fabricated 作形容词：伪造的", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "diagnose", "pos": "verb", "zh": "诊断", "note": "diagnose a disease", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "controversy", "pos": "noun", "zh": "争议", "note": "常与 cause / spark 连用", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "review", "pos": "verb", "zh": "审查", "note": "review rules and procedures", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}], "title_original": "Prize-winning image which sparked backlash was AI-generated, Nikon rules", "summary_original": "The camera-maker says it is now re-evaluating the rules and procedures of its Small World in Motion contest.", "body_original": "Prize-winning image which sparked backlash was AI-generated, Nikon rules\nBy\nZoe Kleinman\nTechnology & AI editor\nPublished\n9 October 2026\nThe winner of a prestigious scientific photography competition has been disqualified following concerns raised by other scientists that it was fake.\nNikon said the use of generative AI in the composition of the video broke the rules of its Small World In Motion contest, which accepts photos and video taken using powerful microscopes.\nThe firm said the video \"did not comply with the competition rules regarding generative AI\", but added it was not a judgement of the entrant's professional reputation or scientific contributions.\nIt said it would now look at the rules and procedures of the annual contest, which has been running for 15 years and is specifically for microscopic images.\nNikon said it came to the decision \"after thoroughly re-evaluating the video and supporting materials and consulting with members of our judging panel\".\nThe winning entry came from Dr Ning Xu, from Tsinghua University in China, who submitted a short video showing tiny, hair-like structures called cilia moving in the airway of a child with the respiratory condition PCD.\nThe BBC has approached him for comment.\nA number of scientists, including former competition judges, complained to Nikon on its LinkedIn page that the video did not appear to be real.\nXu had acknowledged using AI, but said he believed he had acted within the rules, and had cooperated with Nikon's investigation.\nUniversity of Leicester scientist Dr Robert Hirst, who was among the first experts to voice concerns, said he was \"not surprised\" and he \"knew immediately that the video was fabricated\".\n\"I have been diagnosing PCD for 20 years by examining their cilia waveform, length, cell size and shape,\" he said.\nHirst, who is lead scientist for the NHS centre for PCD diagnosis at the university, said the cells and cilia \"look nothing like those from PCD patients\".\n\"This controversy has impacted a lot of patients, scientists, doctors and PCD support networks around the world,\" he said.\nTiny image sparks big backlash in Nikon photo contest\nPublished\n1 October\nWhat is AI, how does it work and why are some people concerned about it?\nPublished\n14 September\nSign up for our Tech Decoded newsletter\nto follow the world's top tech stories and trends.\nOutside the UK? Sign up here\n.\nRelated topics\nArtificial intelligence\nPhotography\nScience & Environment", "fetched_at": "2026-10-12T00:40:13.616651", "timeline": [{"i": 0, "w": "Nikon", "s": 0.1, "e": 0.662, "sm": 100, "em": 662, "cs": 0, "ce": 5, "si": 0}, {"i": 1, "w": "has", "s": 0.675, "e": 0.838, "sm": 675, "em": 837, "cs": 6, "ce": 9, "si": 0}, {"